		"heading": "Deleting",
		"index": 8,
		"text": "Deleting\n\n | Method | Sends | Notes\n\n | delete(memory_id) | DELETE /v1/memories/<id> | One object. Its revision history goes with it.\n\n | delete_by_source(source=None, before=None, after=None, confirm=False) | DELETE /v1/memories | A dry run unless confirm=True. before and after are unix-millisecond bounds.\n\npreview = memory.delete_by_source(source=\"slack-import\")\nprint(preview)\n\n{'ok': True, 'dry_run': True,\n 'would_delete': {'nodes': 3, 'pages': 1, 'slices': 6, 'events': 2}}\n\nRead the preview, then repeat the identical call with confirm=True. A scoped delete (by source, by window, or both) is curation and leaves accepted-but-unfinished writes alone. An unscoped confirmed delete is an erasure: it records a barrier and cancels work already in flight, which is where those cancelled_by_delete jobs come from."
